<A HREF="/msnmain?fn=105&fmt=doc&r=1&pg1=MR"></A>
 <A HREF="/msnmain?fn=705&pg1=CODE&op1=OR&s1=05E35,(33C45,33C80)">05E35 (33C45 33C80)</A>
<BR>
<A HREF="/msnmain?fn=130&fmt=hl&pg1=IID&s1=187605&v1=Zeng%2C%20Jiang">Zeng, Jiang</A><A HREF="/msnmain?fn=505&pg1=CODE&s1=F_STRAS&v1=F-STRAS">(F-STRAS)</A>
<BR>
<strong> Lin&eacute;arisation de produits de polyn&ocirc;mes de Meixner, Krawtchouk, et Charlier.
</strong> <STRONG>(French. English summary)</STRONG>
  [Linearization of products of Meixner, Krawtchouk and Charlier polynomials] 
<BR>
<A HREF="/msnmain?fn=305&pg1=CN&s1=SIAM_J_Math_Anal&v1=SIAM%20J%2E%20Math%2E%20Anal%2E"> <EM>SIAM J. Math. Anal.</EM></A> <STRONG> 21 </STRONG> (1990), <A HREF="/msnmain?fn=130&pg1=ISSI&s1=82827&v1=SIAM%20Journal%20on%20Mathematical%20Analysis%20%5B%2021%20%281990%29%2C%20no%2E%205%5D"> no. 5,</A> 1349--1368. <BR CLEAR=RIGHT>
<HR NOSHADE SIZE="1">Let $(p\sb n(x))\sb {n\geq 0}$ be a sequence of orthogonal polynomials with
respect to a linear functional $L$. The classical problem of
determining information about the linearization coefficients
$\alpha\sb {n,m,k}$, defined by $p\sb n(x)&#183;p\sb m(x)=\sum\sb k\alpha\sb {n,m,k}p\sb k(x)$, or equivalently: $L(p\sb n&#183;p\sb m&#183;p\sb k)=\alpha\sb {n,m,k}&#183;L(p\sb k&#183;p\sb k)$, is of
considerable
interest and has received much attention. More generally, one would
like to evaluate $L(\prod\sp m\sb {i=1}p\sb {n\sb i}(x))$ for any sequence
$\bold
n=(n\sb 1,\cdots,n\sb m)$ of positive integers. In recent years, with the
introduction of combinatorial models for classical orthogonal
polynomials, efforts have been made to approach this problem using
techniques based on combinatorial interpretations. The present article
unifies and extends previous work in this direction.
<P>
For any such $m$-vector $\bold n=(n\sb 1,\cdots,n\sb m)$ let $\bold
A=(A\sb 1,\cdots,A\sb m)$ be a collection of pairwise disjoint sets such
that ${\rm card}A\sb i=n\sb i$ $(1\leq i\leq m)$. A permutation $\pi$ of
$A=A\sb 1\cup\cdots \cup A\sb m$ such that $j\in A\sb i\Rightarrow\pi(j)\notin
A\sb i$ $(j\in A)$ is called a colored derangement of $\bold A$. Let
$D(\bold n;\beta,\gamma)=\sum\sb \pi\beta\sp {{\rm cyc}\,\pi}\gamma\sp {{\rm
exc}\,\pi}$, where $\pi$ runs over all colored derangements of $\bold
A$, ${\rm cyc}\,\pi$ denotes the number of $\pi$-cycles on $A$ in the
usual sense, and ${\rm exc}\,\pi$ gives the number of exceedances of
$\pi$, i.e. the elements $j\in A$ such that $j\in A\sb i$, $\pi(j)\in
A\sb k$ and $i&lt;k$. It is then shown that
$$
D(\bold n,\beta,c\sp {-1})=(-1)\sp {n\sb 1+\cdots+n\sb m}(1-c)\sp \beta
L\Big(\prod\sp m\sb {i=1}M\sb {n\sb i} (x;\beta,c)\Big),
$$
where $L$ is the functional corresponding to the Meixner polynomials
$M\sb n(x;\beta,c)$. (In the case $\beta=1$ this has been shown by R.
A. Askey and M. E.-H. Ismail [Canad. J. Math. 28 (1976),
no. 4, 853--874; <A HREF="/msnmain?fn=105&fmt=doc&r=1&pg1=MR&s1=53:10594">MR <strong>53</strong> #10594</A>].) Similar results are obtained
for the Krawtchouk polynomials (again in terms of the generating
polynomial for colored derangements) and the Charlier polynomials
(using colored partitions instead). From these results assertions
about the positivity of the functionals can be easily obtained
(somewhat less direct in the Krawtchouk case).
<P>
The approach presented here passes via the generating functions of the
structures involved and makes use of the $\beta$-extension of
MacMahon's "master theorem", as obtained by D. Foata and D.
Zeilberger [SIAM J. Discrete Math. 1 (1988), no. 4,
425--433; <A HREF="/msnmain?fn=105&fmt=doc&r=1&pg1=MR&s1=89m:05008">MR 89m:05008</A>]. A number of interesting consequences are
obtained, e.g., in the particular case $m=3$ and for the functionals
related to the Laguerre and Hermite polynomial (by using limiting
relations between these families of orthogonal polynomials).<P><P>
